If \(\left( \frac{a_{1}}{x} \right) + \left( \frac{b_{1}}{y} \right) = c_{1}\) and \(\left( \frac{a_{2}}{x} \right) + \left( \frac{b_{2}}{y} \right) = c_{2}\)
\(\Delta_{1} = \ \left| \begin{matrix}
a_{1} & b_{1} \\
a_{2} & b_{2}
\end{matrix} \right|,\ \Delta_{2} = \left| \begin{matrix}
b_{1} & c_{1} \\
b_{2} & c_{2}
\end{matrix} \right|,\ \Delta_{3} = \left| \begin{matrix}
c_{1} & a_{1} \\
c_{2} & a_{2}
\end{matrix} \right|,\) then, (x, y) is equal to which one of the following?

solution:
Let, \(\frac{1}{x} = x\) and \(\frac{1}{y} = y\)\
     So, equation become,\
     \(a_{1}X + b_{1}Y = c_{1}\)\
     and \(a_{2}X + b_{2}Y = c_{2}\)\
     Then x = \(\frac{\left| \begin{matrix}
     c_{1} & b_{1} \\
     c_{2} & b_{2}
     \end{matrix} \right|}{\left| \begin{matrix}
     a_{1} & b_{1} \\
     a_{2} & b_{2}
     \end{matrix} \right|}\) and \(y = \ \frac{\left| \begin{matrix}
     c_{1} & c_{1} \\
     a_{2} & c_{2}
     \end{matrix} \right|}{\left| \begin{matrix}
     a_{1} & b_{1} \\
     a_{2} & b_{2}
     \end{matrix} \right|}\)\
     \(\frac{1}{x} = \frac{- \Delta_{2}}{\Delta_{1}}\) and \(\frac{1}{y} = \frac{- \Delta_{3}}{\Delta_{1}}\)\
     So, \(x = \frac{- \Delta_{1}}{\Delta_{2}}\ and\ y = \frac{- \Delta_{1}}{\Delta_{3}}\)

